  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA cold front is dropping southeastward through northeast Minnesota\nearly this morning, bringing in a much drier airmass with dewpoints\nfalling into the upper 40s. Patchy dense fog across inland northwest\nWisconsin will last through 5 AM before the front clears it out.\nBehind the front, northwest winds will increase to around 10 mph\nand gust 20 to 25 mph today. This will allow cooler temperatures\nto filter in, returning closer to normal, though still remaining\nslightly above normal for northwest Wisconsin. A weak disturbance\npassing through northwest Ontario later this afternoon and evening\nwill create an area of clouds over the Minnesota Borderlands. Any\nrain showers associated with this feature look to remain north of\nthe International Border.\n\nA colder airmass aloft and building surface high pressure into\nnorth-central Minnesota tonight will create conditions for widespread\nfrost and limited pockets of sub-freezing temperatures into early\nFriday morning. The lingering cloud cover towards midnight may delay\ncooling late this evening for the Minnesota Borderlands, but\ntemperatures are expected to drop quickly as those clouds from the\npassing Ontario disturbance move eastward overnight. A Freeze Warning\nis in effect for the Iron Range and Minnesota Borderlands for late\ntonight into Friday morning. Frost Advisories also cover much of northwest\nWisconsin and other parts of northern Minnesota. Friday will \nfeature dry and seasonable temperatures across the region. \nAnother period of frost and pockets of sub-freezing temperatures\nis expected into early Saturday morning, though more limited in\nareal coverage. Frost headlines may be needed again Friday night for\nthe Arrowhead and much of northwest Wisconsin.\n\nAn incoming shortwave trough passing along the International Border\nlate Friday night will bring increasing scattered rain showers for\nnorth-central Minnesota early Saturday morning. These showers will\nspread eastward into the Arrowhead by daylight Saturday morning. This\nSaturday rainfall has continued to trend northward over the last 24\nhours, with mainly areas of far northern Minnesota north of US\nHighway 2 being affected by light and spotty rain showers through\nSaturday afternoon. A few isolated showers cannot be completely ruled\nout further south into northwest Wisconsin that late afternoon\nand evening though.\n\nThe rain showers will end by Saturday evening as dry weather sets up\nonce again. The next round of even colder air will move in behind an\nincoming cold front on Sunday. Sunday night into Monday morning\nfeatures an even more likely chance of sub-freezing temperatures\nacross the Northland, and freeze headlines will very likely be needed\nto start the next work week. Finally, a warmer air mass moves back\ninto the region from Tuesday through the middle of next week. This\nwill bring a return of areawide above normal temperatures, even\nreaching around 70 degrees by Wednesday, alongside only a 10 percent\nchance of rainfall.",
